// previous: const CACHE = 'aog-cache-2026.09.24.4377'   // BOXES ARE FOR THE SCREEN. ON PAPER THEY ARE A CLAIM, AND THE PAGE HAS NO BUSINESS MAKING IT. Jimmy: "Maybe for the Algorithms, we keep the boxes for the Digital work and get rid of them for the printed version." That is the right cut, and it is the general form of 4376. On the screen a box is an INPUT: the page is asking for a digit, showing where it goes, and it knows how many there are because it holds the answer. On paper the same box is a PREDICTION about working the page has never seen. The answer row claims how many digits the answer has - that was 4376, wrong by one on every sum and product until it was fixed. The CARRY row claims which columns carry: 1980 x 39 printed six carry boxes over a multiplication that carries in three. The PARTIAL-PRODUCT rows claim how many partial products there are and how wide, and they pre-draw the left shift, which is the one thing the standard algorithm exists to teach. Thirty dashed boxes were printing around a single multiplication whose whole working is two lines and an answer, and every box left empty at the end reads to a struggling student as a step missed. A pencil needs less room than a box: a carry is written small, high and left, not centred in a 26px square. SO ON PAPER EVERY BOX INSIDE A COLUMN PROBLEM GOES INVISIBLE - the border and the fill only, never display:none. The element keeps its exact width and height, so the columns, the rules, the START arrow and the page count do not move: measured, 44 sheets across all 11 grades, ZERO page-count differences. What is left is open space in the right place with CARRY, BORROW and THEN ADD still naming what each space is for. The answer row's own decimal point goes with its boxes (class bx, which is only ever the answer row and the quotient line - the points in the PROBLEM stay printed): left in, it is a lone drawn dot floating in white space, which is both meaningless and the exact shape his son already misread