// previous: aog-cache-2026.09.23.4366   // THE DECIMAL POINT WAS SET LIKE A TIMES SIGN. Jimmy sent a photograph of a printed grade-7 sheet his 8th-grade son had worked, with the boy's frustration written across the bottom - "Upper number over / Lower number under / The places have to be in the right slot" - and Jimmy's note: "THE decimal really got him (he thought it was a multiplication sign)." He read it correctly. The point in 3.6 sat in a column of its own, CENTRED in that column and lifted 6px off the baseline, which is exactly how a times dot is set: equidistant between two digits, at their middle. A decimal point is the opposite - it sits at the FEET of the digits and belongs to the one on its LEFT. So the boy read 3 x 6, lined the points up the way you do when adding, and rewrote the whole thing as 6.0 x 3.6. THE FIX: the column stays, because a ruled row draws its border across it; only the glyph moves. It is now drawn rather than typeset - a 7px round dot, because a period at that size renders as a small SQUARE in the system font and a floating square is the very thing he mistook - dropped onto the baseline and pulled left until it is touching the digit before it. A printed digit is a narrow glyph in a wide cell so its point tucks 12px; a row of answer BOXES has no whitespace inside the cell, so those tuck 5px and sit just off the box edge. Second thing, because the boy's note was about alignment and not only about the dot: a multiplication that HAS a point in it now carries one line - "line up the ends, not the points" - which is the rule he got wrong. Nothing else gains a word. SWEPT, not assumed: 660 math sheets across every grade, 1984 column problems and 307 long divisions. 283 column problems and 112 divisions carry a printed point; every single one is the new drawn dot, zero left in the old shape. 107 decimal multiplications, every one labelled, and no label on a problem without a point. Decimals live in grades 3, 5, 6 and 7 (money at 3). Zero generator errors. Page counts compared before and after across 18 sheets in grades 3 to 8: no change anywhere. No box overflows its column and no label is clipped (AOG-DD-DECPOINT-V1, daily-drops.html).
